---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro'
import EnvTable from '../components/EnvTable.astro'
import Step from '../components/Step.astro'
import { parseEnvExample, readRepoFile } from '../lib/repo'

const compose = readRepoFile('docker/compose.yaml')
const entries = parseEnvExample(readRepoFile('docker/.env.example'))
---
<Page
  title="Install PSPad"
  description="Run PSPad on your own hardware with Docker Compose: the stack, every environment variable, the secrets, and the first sign-in."
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Install</h1>
    <p>
      PSPad runs as four containers: the API, the client, MongoDB, and Keycloak for
      sign-in. One Compose file starts all four. Budget several minutes, not seconds,
      especially on a machine that has never pulled the base images before.
    </p>

    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Docker with Compose v2 (<code>docker compose version</code> prints v2.x).</li>
      <li>About 2 GB of disk for images and data.</li>
      <li>
        For anything beyond <code>localhost</code>: a reverse proxy terminating TLS.
        The stack serves plain HTTP on purpose and does not manage certificates.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <Step n={1} title="Clone the repository">
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/psp515/PSPad.git
cd PSPad</code></pre>
    </Step>

    <Step n={2} title="Create your environment file">
      <pre><code>cp docker/.env.example docker/.env</code></pre>
      <p>
        Do this before anything else. <code>docker/compose.yaml</code> reads every
        <code>${'{VAR}'}</code> from <code>docker/.env</code>; without it, <code>docker compose up</code>
        starts containers with empty or missing values instead of failing loudly. The
        defaults work on <code>localhost</code> as they are. Change at least
        <code>MONGO_PASSWORD</code> and <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> before the stack is
        reachable from anywhere else.
      </p>
      <EnvTable entries={entries} />
      <Callout kind="warning" title="Two Keycloak URLs, and they mean different things">
        <p>
          <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code> is the realm URL the <strong>browser</strong> uses —
          the WebAssembly client redirects there to sign in. <code>KEYCLOAK_INTERNAL_AUTHORITY</code>
          is the realm URL the <strong>API container</strong> uses to validate tokens. They have
          to differ because the two reach Keycloak by different names: inside the <code>api</code>
          container, <code>localhost</code> means that container, not the host machine, so the
          browser's address does not work there. Running your own external Keycloak instead of
          this stack's bundled one? Set both variables to whatever address the API container can
          actually reach that instance at — often the same public URL as <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code>.
          Leaving <code>KEYCLOAK_INTERNAL_AUTHORITY</code> at its default with no <code>keycloak</code>
          service running makes every authenticated API call fail.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <Callout title="The admin credentials apply once">
        <p>
          <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER</code> and <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> create the
          Keycloak administrator on the very first start. Changing them later has no
          effect — change the password in the Keycloak console instead.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <Step n={3} title="Start the stack">
      <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
      <p>
        The first build took a bit over a minute with the .NET SDK and ASP.NET base images
        already cached locally; pulling those images for the first time adds several minutes
        on top. MongoDB starts as a single-node replica set — PSPad writes each command, its
        events and its command id in one transaction, and transactions require a replica set.
        Wait for it to report healthy:
      </p>
      <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml ps</code></pre>
      <p>
        The API will not start until Mongo is healthy. On the first run Mongo also
        generates its own replica-set keyfile into the <code>mongo-keyfile</code> volume.
        <code>api</code>, <code>app</code> and <code>keycloak</code> declare no healthcheck of their
        own, so <code>ps</code> shows them as a bare <code>Up</code> forever — that is expected,
        not a sign anything is wrong. Confirm the API is actually answering instead:
      </p>
      <pre><code>curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{'{'}http_code{'}'}\n" http://localhost:5000/api/today</code></pre>
      <p>
        An unauthenticated request should come back <code>401</code>. Anything else —
        <code>500</code>, <code>000</code>, a hang — means the stack did not come up cleanly.
      </p>
      <Callout title="The API runs in Production mode, on purpose">
        <p>
          <code>docker compose exec api printenv ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code> prints nothing,
          and the API logs <code>Hosting environment: Production</code>. It can still reach the
          bundled Keycloak over plain HTTP because <code>docker/compose.yaml</code> sets
          <code>Keycloak__RequireHttpsMetadata: "false"</code> explicitly for the <code>api</code>
          service — not because it is running in Development mode. If you later put an identity
          provider behind HTTPS, change that value to <code>"true"</code> in
          <code>docker/compose.yaml</code> rather than setting <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code>,
          which would also turn on the developer exception page and leak stack traces to anyone
          who can reach the API.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <Step n={4} title="Create your first user">
      <p>
        PSPad has no registration screen; accounts live in Keycloak, and the realm ships
        with none. <strong>Creating one now is mandatory, not optional</strong> — the
        application is unusable until you do.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open the admin console at the <code>KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME</code> you configured
          (<code>http://localhost:8080</code> by default) and sign in with the bootstrap
          administrator (<code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER</code> / <code>KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          Switch realm from <strong>master</strong> to <strong>pspad</strong>. A fresh stack's
          Users list reads "No users found" — that is correct.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Users</strong> → <strong>Create new user</strong>. Set a username and email,
          and fill in first and last name while you are there — doing it now skips an
          "Update Account Information" interstitial that would otherwise interrupt the very
          first sign-in.
        </li>
        <li>
          Create lands on a separate detail view for that user. <strong>Credentials</strong> →
          <strong>Set password</strong> is a second, separate action — with <em>Temporary</em>
          switched off, or Keycloak forces a password change the client has no screen for.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </Step>

    <Step n={5} title="Sign in">
      <p>
        Open <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code>. You are redirected to Keycloak, sign in, and
        land on My Day — empty at first, showing "Nothing due today." while the app
        provisions three starter areas (Personal, Work, Home) in the background; they can
        take a few seconds to appear. Install it from the browser's address bar to get the
        offline app — from then on it keeps working with no network and syncs when one returns.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="warning" title="Give it 20–30 seconds before you trust something is saved">
        <p>
          The client is offline-first: creating an area, a list or a task applies to a
          local browser-side replica immediately, and is only queued for delivery to the
          server in the background on a periodic interval — observed around 20–30 seconds.
          If you create something and immediately restart the stack or close the browser
          within that window, it was never actually written to MongoDB and is lost.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <h2>The stack, in full</h2>
    <p>This is <code>docker/compose.yaml</code> exactly as it ships:</p>
    <pre><code>{compose}</code></pre>

    <h2>Putting it behind a proxy</h2>
    <p>
      Point three hostnames at the published ports — the client on <code>5001</code>, the API
      on <code>5000</code>, Keycloak on <code>8080</code> — terminate TLS at the proxy, and forward
      <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>. Then:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Set <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code>, <code>API_BASE_ADDRESS</code>,
        <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code> and <code>KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME</code> in
        <code>docker/.env</code> to those public <code>https://</code> URLs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Edit the Keycloak client, or sign-in will fail.</strong> Keycloak 26 rejects
        <code>${'{env.…}'}</code> placeholders in a realm import, so the bundled realm export
        carries the literal <code>http://localhost:5001/*</code> redirect URI baked in — changing
        <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code> does not change it. In the admin console, realm
        <strong>pspad</strong> → <strong>Clients</strong> → <strong>pspad-app</strong>, update
        <strong>Valid redirect URIs</strong> to your new public address. <strong>Web origins</strong>
        needs no separate edit — it ships as <code>+</code>, Keycloak's shorthand for "derive the
        allowed origins from Valid redirect URIs," so it follows along automatically once that
        field is correct, unless you have changed it yourself. Skip the redirect URI edit and
        sign-in fails with <code>Invalid parameter: redirect_uri</code> and nothing on screen
        explains why.
      </li>
      <li>Recreate the stack so the new environment values take effect:
        <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --force-recreate</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      MongoDB publishes no port at all; it is reachable only from the other containers.
    </p>

    <h2>Backups</h2>
    <p>Three volumes hold everything that matters:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>pspad_mongo-data</code> — your areas, lists, tasks and the event log.</li>
      <li><code>pspad_mongo-keyfile</code> — the generated replica-set key. Restoring <code>mongo-data</code> without it leaves a set that will not start.</li>
      <li><code>pspad_keycloak-data</code> — accounts and realm configuration.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A dump of the database alone:</p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml exec -T mongo \
  sh -c 'mongodump --archive --username pspad --password "$MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD" --authenticationDatabase admin' \
  > pspad-backup.archive</code></pre>
    <p>
      Single quotes around the inner <code>sh -c</code> command are deliberate: the password has
      to come from <code>MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD</code>, which only exists inside the
      <code>mongo</code> container's own environment. Double quotes would let your host shell try
      to expand <code>$MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD</code> itself — where it is unset — and
      authenticate with an empty password instead.
    </p>

    <h2>Upgrading, restarting</h2>
    <pre><code>git pull
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
    <p>
      Data survives: the volumes are not touched. <code>down -v</code> is the command that
      destroys them. A restart — <code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml restart</code>,
      or an upgrade's rebuild — brings every container down at once, and the API refuses
      connections until Mongo reports healthy again. Give it 10–30 seconds before you judge
      the stack broken; that was enough on every run observed during verification.
    </p>

    <h2>Two things worth knowing</h2>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Keycloak runs in development mode">
      <p>
        It stores its data in an embedded H2 database on the <code>keycloak-data</code> volume.
        That is deliberate — a PostgreSQL container for a single-user deployment whose
        entire store is one MongoDB is not a trade worth making — but Keycloak does not
        support this mode for production, and a major version bump of the image may not
        migrate cleanly. Export the realm before bumping it.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="The old keyfile is in the git history">
      <p>
        Early revisions of this repository committed <code>docker/mongo-keyfile</code>. Your
        installation generates its own on first start and never uses that one, but the
        original value cannot be unpublished. It is only usable by someone who can already
        reach your MongoDB, which publishes no port.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Page>
